{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbUBgbvUPogBa9PPGYEN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written all three files for candidate 2, and the self-check passes: `OK`, ELPD-LOO −2587.9 on its 100-draw smoke fit. That fit is short, and the check doesn't test novelty against the other models, R-hat on multiple chains or the full fit's convergence. It probably won't top the model set: −2587.9 is about 290 nats behind the best model (−2301.4). This is an exploratory slot, though, and the mechanism is different from every model in the set.\n\n**Model name:** `run_rhythm_evidence_personal_coin`\n\n**Hypothesis:** people judge randomness by Bayesian model comparison. They ask whether a coin explains the sequence better than a \"regular-rhythm\" generator that builds sequences from runs of a few recurring lengths. So sequences whose runs all share one length (HTHTHTHT, HHTTHHTT, one solid streak) look designed, and sequences with varied run lengths look random. This targets one of the critique's two discrepancies that survive the FDR: people choose the sequence with more distinct run lengths more often than the current best model predicts. The brief asked for one distortion of a normative account. Here it is that each person's mental fair coin switches between heads and tails at their own rate instead of exactly half the time.\n\n**How the model works:**\n- **Coin side:** the coin's log-likelihood comes from the sequence's switch and repeat counts at the person's own switch rate.\n- **Rhythm side:** the rhythm generator's log-likelihood treats the run lengths as draws from a distribution the person doesn't know. It has one shared parameter, how strongly that generator expects a single recurring run length.\n- **Choice:** people pick by comparing the two sequences' evidence, scaled by a personal decisiveness, and they guess on some trials at a personal rate. Person-level parameters are drawn from population distributions, with 400 slots so the next design can predict new participants.\n\n**Speed fix:** my first version was too slow for the 30-minute admission limit (about 8 minutes for 200 iterations). I rewrote it to build a table of the distinct sequences and compute the expensive rhythm term once per sequence instead of once per trial. That cut the time to about 1 minute.\n\nFiles are in `experiment2/model_loop/iter_3/candidate_2/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7882,"cache_read_input_tokens":85549,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7882,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e90d422-c55d-4759-ade1-22ce92f76aaa","uuid":"bb8c6c2b-4bae-4627-9fe1-495c29017f29","timestamp":"2026-10-01T11:52:17.085Z","request_id":"req_011CfbUBfQjj3gDuZSNYArNp"}
